                        "glosses": [
                            "That tends to prevent the development of something, especially a disease."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dated"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3d9019b9-c049-4b9a-8d5e-543ec15749a9",
                                "sense_id": "f531b8c7-2493-33b1-a83b-1215a743c91d",
                                "text": "In this case none of the symptoms were present, and there can be no doubt that the ectrotic treatment really checked the progress of suppuration and modified the disease."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f80fe42b-050c-4084-ab0c-5059fe7a61c5",
                                "sense_id": "f531b8c7-2493-33b1-a83b-1215a743c91d",
                                "text": "In both sexes the ectrotic treatment of applying nitrate of silver to the inflamed surface, either in stick or in strong solution, is undesirable, being fraught with untoward results."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5e1ecb52-50e3-426d-b0c5-16f5179f2f3a",
                                "sense_id": "f531b8c7-2493-33b1-a83b-1215a743c91d",
                                "text": "1892, William M. Welch, Small-pox, Hobart Amory Hare, Walter Chrystie, A System of Practical Therapeutics, Volume II, Lea Brothers & Co., page 257,\nIf any ectrotic measure were reliable, how easy it would be to limit the amount of cutaneous inflammation, to lessen, if not prevent, the so-called secondary fever, and thus obviate the danger from exhaustion."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
